 ## Overview
-Projection-Based Ray Casting is a direct volume rendering technique for curvilinear hexahedral grids that determines cell traversal by projecting cell faces onto the 2D image plane [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently finding entry and exit points in irregular, non-planar meshes by avoiding complex 3D geometric intersection tests for every cell, instead relying on 2D screen-space projection and ray-crossing tests [S1, unofficial]. Unlike general traversal methods that may enter and exit internal boundaries or require pre-computed topological structures, this method leverages the associativity of compositing and on-the-fly decomposition, making it suitable for distributed-memory architectures and irregular sampling densities [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Projection-Based Ray Casting is a direct volume rendering technique for curvilinear hexahedral grids that determines cell traversal by projecting cell faces onto the 2D image plane [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently finding entry and exit points in irregular, non-planar meshes by avoiding complex 3D geometric intersection tests for every cell, instead relying on 2D screen-space projection and ray-crossing tests [S1, unofficial]. Unlike general traversal methods that require pre-computed topological structures, this method uses on-the-fly decomposition and 2D projection, making it suitable for irregular sampling densities [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
